prompt:
Predict which option this user would choose. If no personal evidence is provided, judge what a typical user would prefer. Return {"probabilities": [one probability per option, in order, summing to 1], "rationale": "short reason"}.
INPUT_JSON:
{"context": "A research team is auditing an airline customer-service agent benchmark for flawed tasks that may deflate measured capability. To scale the audit, they gave LLM judges the full prompt, agent log, agent submission, and gold answer (without the grading result) and asked whether each flagged result should have been graded as correct. They must now decide whether the judges' flags can be treated as genuine task errors.\n\nQuestion: Under what conditions should the team accept the LLM judges' flags of flawed tasks as valid evidence of benchmark errors?", "options": ["Manually check every task the automated judges flag and accept a flag as a genuine benchmark error only after human verification.", "Treat flags as valid when the judge cites a specific contradiction with stated policy, auditing a random sample to estimate the false-flag rate."], "principles": [], "persona": "An AI safety researcher or technical forecaster who prizes epistemic rigor, precise conceptual distinctions, and methodological soundness. They insist on clear definitional boundaries—scheming vs. reward hacking, distinct task capabilities vs. single capability estimates—over tidy collapsible taxonomies. They favor theory-first reasoning (coverage claims need defined criteria) paired with concrete, mechanism-focused empirical evidence over speculative arguments. They are scope-disciplined: they prefer tractable, well-founded analyses over ambitious extensions built on noisy inputs, and conservative, hedged forecasts that flag model limitations over confident headline numbers. On alignment risk, they take deceptive alignment seriously as an incentive-structure concern while remaining cautious about unproven claims, favoring layered defenses (control, interpretability, oversight), mechanism-aware measurement, and mitigations that don't merely train models to hide the measured signal. They distrust lab-to-deployment extrapolation, preferring real-world data even if noisy. They are skeptical of low-evidence-value experiments with confounded interpretations, preferring controlled baselines, non-IID generalization tests, and diagnostics that cleanly distinguish hypotheses. They value conceptual generality (architecture-agnostic interpretability, universal learning dynamics) and calm, accessible public communication.", "past_choices": []}
